<h2>Are Christmas Lights Safe for Cats?</h2>
<p>Used normally, string lights are not a routine hazard for most cats, and plenty of households decorate every year without an incident. The real risk shows up with a specific behavior: chewing. Cats explore with their mouths, and a dangling cord moving slightly as the lights warm up can look like a toy. PetMD's overview of electrocution in pets names chewing through electrical cords as a leading cause of electric shock injuries in cats and dogs, cords included. The lights are not the hazard; an exposed, unsecured cord within reach of a chewing cat is.</p>
<p>That makes the fix straightforward: it is less about which lights you buy and more about where the cord sits and whether it is live when nobody is watching.</p>

<h2>Can Cats Get Electrocuted by Christmas Lights?</h2>
<p>Yes, though it is uncommon. A cat that bites through a live cord can receive an electric shock, and veterinary sources including Cornell and PetMD describe burns to the mouth and tongue, drooling, and in more serious cases, fluid building up in the lungs (pulmonary edema) that can cause labored breathing. That breathing trouble does not always show up immediately; it can develop hours after the bite, which is why a vet check matters even if your cat seems fine right after chewing a cord.</p>
<p>Vetster's guide to electric cord bite injuries in cats lists the signs to watch for after any cord-chewing incident: burns or redness inside the mouth, drooling, reluctance to eat, coughing, and labored or rapid breathing. Any of these after a cord bite is a reason to call your vet, and breathing trouble on its own is an emergency.</p>

